        "type": "thinking",
        "thinking": "I'm trying to recall whether the root कुण with the meaning \"to invite/summon\" (आमन्त्रणे) belongs to the curādi gaṇa in the Dhatupatha, cross-checking it against neighboring entries like कूण (to contract) and तूण (to fill).\n\nI'm continuing through the list of ātmanepadī roots in this group—vañcu, vṛṣa, mada, divu, gṝ, vida, māna, yu, kusma—before moving into the kathādi group with roots like aṅka, sukha, rasa, vyaya, and rūpa.\n\nI'm continuing to recall more dhātus in this list — pada for movement, gṛha for grasping, mṛga for seeking, kuha for astonishment, śūra for valor, sthūla for expanding, artha for requesting, satra for continuity, garva for pride, sūtra for enveloping, mūtra for excreting, rūkṣa for roughness, pāra/tīra for completing a task, puṭa for mixing, dheka for seeing, katra for looseness, valka for speaking, and more continuing into chip.\n\nContinuing through more of this dhatu list—sार, स्पृह, भाम, सूच, खेट, गोम, कुमार, शील, साम, वेल, काल, पल्यूल, वात, गवेष, वास, निवास, भाज, सभाज—each with their traditional meanings like weakness, desire, anger, slander, play, protection, and seeking.\n\nI'm tracing the ātmanepadī gaṇa through \"garva\" and then the parasmaipadī group starting with \"sūtra veṣṭane,\" while trying to place the kathādi list in the correct sequence before or after it.\n\nFor kuṇa specifically, since it's an adanta root, the upadhā is ṇ rather than u, so laghūpadha guṇa doesn't apply—instead ato lopa deletes the final a, and sthānivadbhāva blocks guṇa from applying there.\n\nSo the resulting form is कुणयति, giving लुट् as कुणयितारौ—paralleling the well-known गुणयति (to multiply) from the related root गुण, as Siddhanta Kaumudi groups \"कुण गुण च आमन्त्रणे\" together.\n\n",
        "signature": ""
      },
